      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": { "level": 2 },
        "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Finishing" }]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "You will have four tails to weave in: the colour A magic ring tail, the colour A fasten-off tail at the end of round 2, and the colour B join tail and fasten-off tail. Weave each tail through 3 to 4 stitches on the wrong side, reverse direction, and trim close. Steam-block flat on a blocking mat."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": { "level": 2 },
        "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Joining" }]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The two-colour square joins at its four corner ch-2 spaces and its 12 side ch-1 spaces, in the same way as any four-round granny. For a blanket where the colour pattern matters, lay out the squares before joining to check the two-colour arrangement reads consistently — a half-turn in one square flips which colour lands at the outer edge."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": { "level": 2 },
        "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Care" }]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Handwash in cool water, or machine-wash on a delicates cycle. Check that both colours are colourfast before washing finished projects together. Lay flat to dry."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": { "level": 2 },
        "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "What to try next" }]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The three-colour version takes the same clean-break logic one step further: colour A in round 1, colour B in rounds 2 and 3, colour C in round 4. The five-round version uses the same two-colour split but with more rounds per colour band."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
